3. go-libucl
------------

    Upstream:  https://github.com/mitchellh/go-libucl
    Version:   commit f9d0e552356c1708096029bcfc05dfb0e3a3bd56 (2015-11-19)
    Licence:   none stated — see the caveat below
    Author:    Mitchell Hashimoto <xmitchx@gmail.com>

compat/libucl reproduces the API of Mitchell Hashimoto's cgo binding so that
programs written against it can drop the C toolchain unchanged. The design,
the API shape, the `libucl` struct tag, the type and constant names and the
method sets are all his work, carried over deliberately.

Four files in compat/libucl come from that repository rather than from this
project:

    decoder.go ........ byte-identical to upstream
    object_test.go .... byte-identical to upstream
    parser_test.go .... byte-identical to upstream
    decoder_test.go ... differs only by the comment markers disabling one
                        test that is broken upstream; the evidence is
                        recorded beside it in the file

compat.go and decoder_flaky_test.go are this project's own.

    CAVEAT: the go-libucl repository carries no LICENSE file, no per-file
    copyright headers, and no licence statement in its README, and it is
    archived on GitHub with no instruction about reuse. A request to its
    author asking for clarification went unanswered, so the question is
    settled only in the sense that it will not be answered. Attribution is
    therefore given here as credit, and credit is not a licence grant. Anyone
    redistributing goucl with compat/libucl included should treat the
    upstream-derived files above as unlicensed third-party code and decide
    deliberately whether to carry them.

    The BSD 2-clause terms in the root LICENSE cover the goucl module and do
    not extend to compat/libucl. That module states its own position in
    compat/libucl/LICENSE: its terms are whatever the original library's are,
    which is to say unstated. It is deliberately not placed under this
    project's licence, because this project cannot license files it does not
    own.

    Of the four, only decoder.go is shipped code; the other three are the
    test suite that proves this package is a faithful drop-in. That split
    matters if permission is not forthcoming: decoder.go can be reimplemented
    as original work, and the tests are what would demonstrate that the
    reimplementation still behaves identically.

    compat/libucl is therefore a separate Go module. The main goucl module
    contains none of these files and does not depend on them; this package is
    required explicitly by the programs that need it, and can be released,
    held back or withdrawn without touching the rest.


Not derived from
----------------

Recorded so that nobody re-adds attribution that is not owed, and so the
reasoning is not rediscovered:

  * mum.h (c) 2016 Vladimir Makarov — libucl hashes keys with mum_hash.
    internal/core/hash.go does not: it pairs a Go map with an explicit
    insertion-order slice, because insertion order is the property the
    goldens depend on and the bucket order is not observable.

  * klib khash.h / kvec.h (c) 2008-2011 Attractive Chaos, MIT — libucl's hash
    and array storage. The Go uses native maps and slices. kvec appears in
    internal/core only as a comment explaining an observable null-pointer
    behaviour, not as translated code.

  * tree.h (c) 2005 Ian Piumarta — libucl's ucl_schema.c builds a binary
    search tree for uniqueItems. internal/core/schema.go compares directly
    instead, which is a documented divergence rather than a translation.
